{
  "schema_version": "formalization-faithfulness-1",
  "role": "adjudicator",
  "task_id": "LEV-CH01-ADVECTION-LINEAR-FLUX-CANONICAL-20260908",
  "trigger": [
    "judge classifications differ",
    "round-trip judge requested adjudication",
    "round-trip judge classification is undetermined",
    "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C04 unclear",
    "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C10 unclear",
    "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item N06 unclear",
    "round-trip judge left implication source_implies_translation unclear"
  ],
  "resolved_items": [
    {
      "item": "judge classifications differ",
      "resolution": "Classify the target as not-faithful-weaker against the complete selected contaminant paragraph. The homogeneous classical implication is represented, but the explicit requirement for source terms when contaminant mass is not conserved is absent.",
      "primary_evidence": "Attached raw PDF page 26, printed page 4, contaminant paragraph includes the nonconservation case. The elaborated target concludes only the constructed flux identity and the scalar advection predicate; D001 assumes homogeneous interval conservation."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge requested adjudication",
      "resolution": "The request identifies two distinct matters: an actual omitted source case and a question about the interpretation of unspecified smoothness. The omission is substantive. The displayed analytical premises adequately instantiate the source's sufficient-regularity proviso for the represented derivation.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 4 contains both the source-term qualification and the opening of section 1.1.2. The target separately assumes derivative existence, interval integrability, differentiation under the integral, and residual continuity."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge classification is undetermined",
      "resolution": "Resolve the final classification to not-faithful-weaker, with acceptance false. This does not certify equality of all possible regularity classes that could be associated with the source's informal wording; such equality is neither stated by the source nor required to recognize this explicit sufficient formulation.",
      "primary_evidence": "Section 1.1.2 specifies sufficient smoothness without fixing a function space or asserting minimal assumptions. The target supplies conditions for the same integral-to-differential consequence, while omitting a separate conclusion from the selected paragraph."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C04 unclear",
      "resolution": "Resolve C04 as pass for the homogeneous classical derivation. Joint continuous differentiability is sufficient to supply the target's analytical conditions on bounded intervals. The target does not assert that its conditions imply joint continuous differentiability. Its weaker, operational regularity package explicitly justifies the manipulations needed here and imposes no sign or nonzero-speed restriction.",
      "primary_evidence": "The target's hqt, hqx, and hfluxx concern actual slice derivatives. hqtIntegrable and hfluxxIntegrable concern bounded spatial intervals; hinterchange explicitly supplies differentiation under their integrals; hresidualContinuous supplies the continuity needed for a pointwise conclusion. Printed page 4, section 1.1.2, leaves the sufficient conditions unspecified."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C10 unclear",
      "resolution": "Resolve C10 as pass for scalar representation and the explicit classical formulation. Evaluation at the unique element of Fin 1 reverses the scalar embedding. The source's inherited real space and time domains are preserved. The universally quantified selected time is not a special-time restriction. Neither additional premises nor the use of generic supporting definitions establishes genuine theorem strength.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 1 gives real space and time and the scalar case m=1. D005 maps q(x,t) to its single coordinate; D002 is the matrix [speed]. D001 quantifies over every real time, while the target's remaining analytical assumptions and conclusion concern an arbitrary real t."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item N06 unclear",
      "resolution": "Resolve N06 as pass. The theorem assumes sufficient analytical conditions and concludes a classical equation for a given q. The existential witnesses are derivative values, not newly constructed solutions. There is no existence, uniqueness, invertibility, or well-posedness assertion beyond the represented claim.",
      "primary_evidence": "D007 explicitly quantifies two derivative values and requires their HasDerivAt predicates and zero residual. The target universally quantifies q. The selected source pages state no initial-boundary-value existence or uniqueness result for this claim."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left implication source_implies_translation unclear",
      "resolution": "Resolve this direction as yes under the source's unspecified sufficient-smoothness proviso. This uses an explicit sufficient formulation of that proviso, not an assertion that two fixed regularity classes are equal. The same interval balance and the displayed analytical premises yield the source's classical differential consequence, and the prescribed singleton matrix gives its exact flux formula.",
      "primary_evidence": "D001 and hinterchange identify the integral of qt with F(Q(a,t))-F(Q(b,t)). The actual spatial flux derivative and its interval integrability identify the integral of fluxx with the opposite endpoint difference. Thus the continuous residual has zero integral on every interval and vanishes pointwise. D002, D003, D094, and hqx identify fluxx with speed times qx. These are precisely the operations connecting source equations (1.10), (1.8), and (1.2)."
    },
    {
      "item": "Disputed dependency meanings and task-specific reuse effects",
      "resolution": "The supplied declaration bodies confirm the relevant interpretations independently of reuse hashes. D001 preserves interval integrability, all-time scope, and left-minus-right endpoint signs. D004 unfolds to D007, which requires actual scalar partial derivatives and their exact advection equation. D006 and D008 supply structural continuity of scalar multiplication, not regularity assumptions on q. No disputed dependency changes the represented equation.",
      "primary_evidence": "The complete inline inventory supplies the bodies of D001-D005 and D007, the explicit types of D006 and D008, and the derivative, integral, matrix, arithmetic, and topology declarations. D024 uses the neighborhood of the differentiation point; D026 and D090 preserve oriented interval integration; D096 supplies multiplication-based real scalar action."
    },
    {
      "item": "Conclusion completeness and exceptional-case consistency",
      "resolution": "C05 fails because the nonconservation/source-term assertion is missing. C11 also fails if it includes coverage of this explicitly stated exceptional case, as its supplied requirement does. This is the same omission, not a second independent mathematical defect. Assuming conservation prevents an unqualified homogeneous conclusion, but does not establish the required model in the nonconserved case.",
      "primary_evidence": "The final sentences of the selected paragraph on printed page 4 require source terms when contaminant mass is not conserved. Neither the target's two conclusion conjuncts nor D001-D008 represents that case or a source term."
    },
    {
      "item": "Nonvacuity, exact flux, and purported strength",
      "resolution": "The assumptions admit nonconstant smooth transported profiles and are not contradictory. For example, q(x,s)=exp(-(x-speed*s)^2), with its actual derivative fields, satisfies the interval balance and the analytical premises. Fin 1 is inhabited, and all endpoint pairs are quantified. The exact flux identity is an algebraic identification of the prescribed flux; it is not a derivation of an unknown constitutive law or evidence of stronger scope.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 1, equation (1.3), describes translated profiles. D002-D003 and D094 give the singleton matrix product speed*state(0), with no additive constant. D001 and D026 explicitly require integrability, avoiding reliance on totalized nonintegrable integrals."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "lean_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "no",
      "reasoning": "The target represents the exact linear flux and the homogeneous classical conservation-to-advection implication. It does not represent the complete selected paragraph's assertion that nonconserved contaminant mass requires source terms. Its conservation premise excludes that case without supplying the source's conclusion about it."
    },
    "source_implies_lean": {
      "verdict": "yes",
      "reasoning": "The source specifies f(r)=ū*r and derives the scalar advection equation from interval conservation subject to sufficient smoothness. The target gives explicit sufficient conditions for those same classical operations: actual derivatives, interval integrability, differentiation under the integral, and continuity of the residual. Under the reversible singleton encoding these yield precisely the two target conjuncts. This interpretation does not assert equality with an unstated minimal regularity class or infer the result from kernel acceptance."
    }
  },
  "classification": "not-faithful-weaker",
  "accepted": false,
  "remaining_uncertainties": [
    "The source does not specify a minimal regularity class. No equality between the target's admissible class and every possible interpretation of sufficient smoothness is established; the adjudication recognizes the displayed package as an adequate explicit sufficient formulation."
  ],
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "omitted-source-case",
      "evidence": "Attached printed page 4 explicitly requires source terms for nonconserved contaminant mass. The complete target assumes homogeneous conservation and concludes only the prescribed flux identity and homogeneous scalar advection.",
      "impact": "The target is incomplete against the complete selected paragraph. Acceptance is withheld."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "regularity-interpretation",
      "evidence": "Section 1.1.2 leaves sufficient smoothness unspecified. The target explicitly supplies the analytical conditions for differentiation, interval integration, interchange, and passage from vanishing interval residuals to pointwise equality.",
      "impact": "The regularity question does not establish an additional faithfulness defect or justify a stronger classification. No claim of minimality or exact function-space equivalence is made."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Inspection of the attached primary-source pages and inline declaration evidence confirms the scalar domain, constant coefficient, exact flux, endpoint orientation, actual derivative semantics, and nonvacuity. The blind translation accurately reconstructs the target. The regularity disagreement is resolved by distinguishing an adequate explicit sufficient formulation from equality of unspecified regularity classes. The decisive failure is the missing nonconservation/source-term case. Source and rendering hashes are treated as supplied provenance; they were not independently recomputed."
}
